AB  - The preliminary study of recycled Polyethylene Terephthalate (rPET) reinforced Empty Fruit Brunch (EFB) fibers as a potential biodegradable 3-dimensional (3D) printing filament material was investigated through melt flow viscosity analysis. 10 wt% EFB fiber was blended with 90 wt% rPET using internal mixer until steady state condition achieved. The melt flow index (MFI) of rPET/EFB composite was tested at 250℃ to 280℃. The addition of 10 wt% EFB fiber increased the MFI value of rPET/EFB composite compared to rPET at all temperatures significantly. rPET/EFB composite starts flowing at lower temperature which is at 250℃ at 31.271 g/10 min compared to rPET at 260℃ at 0.324 g/10 min. Thus, the activation energy of rPET/EFB composite is lower compared to rPET which is 216.91 J/mol and 431.94 J/mol respectively. The findings indicate the possibility of rPET/EFB composite to be 3D printed at lower operating temperature.
