AB  - Given the current paradigm shift regarding environmental issues, the search for sustainable solutions and the urgency to rethink processes of production of goods and services, the present work aims to present a process of recycling additive manufacturing residues (3D printing) for development of geometric models for the learning, in the basic education, of the concepts of volumetry and recognition of geometric forms. The recycling process proposed in this experiment consists of 5 parts, namely: (1) Selection step, (2) Fragmentation stage, (3) Melting stage, (4) Machining step, and (6) Development stage presenting the parameters for the production of the pieces (quantity of raw material, temperature and duration) to improve the finishing and the variables for cutting and engraving laser (power and speed). The experiment was documented in detail and analyzed for the systematization of procedures, aiming to serve as a reference for future applications in the field of geometry teaching and product design. Through the presentation of the results - the production of a didactic model for teaching geometry - we sought to contribute to the recycling of plastic waste in rapid prototyping laboratories and Makerspaces, combining the manual making of digital technologies.
