AB  - The amount of marine plastic litter in the ocean poses a major threat to the environment, especially coastal communities. High-Density Polyethylene (HDPE), a durable plastic widely used in marine applications like fishing lines, nets, and mooring cables, is a significant contributor to marine pollution. This research explores the potential of repurposing maritime activity-derived HDPE waste for use in the Architecture, Engineering and Construction (AEC) industries through additive manufacturing technologies, such as 3D printing. The study also researches the source of the waste, detailing the process of cleaning, extrusion, and its conversion into 3D-printable filaments and pellets. Small-scale 3D-printed prototypes with 100% HDPE recovered waste were fabricated to evaluate the material’s feasibility for architectural components. By leveraging these 3DP techniques, this study aims to transform waste into a valuable circular material for regenerative architectural applications. The paper provides a comprehensive analysis of the material processing steps, the challenges of using recycled plastics in digital manufacturing, and the broader implications of integrating circular materials into the construction sector. Additionally, it addresses the scalability of such solutions, offering a framework for future exploration of how digital technologies can drive circularity in architecture and design. By rethinking waste as a resource and harnessing advanced manufacturing techniques, this study contributes to a more sustainable and regenerative approach to building materials in the context of a circular economy. This research not only demonstrates the feasibility of transforming marine plastic waste into functional architectural materials but also promotes a shift toward more resilient and eco-conscious design practices through the integration of digital technologies and circular resource management strategies.
