AB  - An increasing demand for carbon-neutral, sustainable alternatives to petrochemical products has invigorated interests around the industrial utilization of biomass and biomass-derived feedstocks.1, 2 In addition, a closed-loop system in which feed materials and energy are recovered or recycled has been identified as a key framework for facilitating the transition to eco-effective industrial systems, thereby encouraging its widespread adoption across industrial sectors based on different bioresources.1, 2 Seaweeds have been a key subject of extensive investigations with respect to facilitating circular biomass-based economy because of its clear advantages over terrestrial crops.2, 3 In particular, the cultivation potential of seaweeds in large-scale offshore farms has promoted them as a promising candidate for carbon capture with minimal external nutrient requirements and almost zero competition for agricultural land.3, 4 Moreover, seaweeds themselves have considerable economic value not just because of their fast growth rates, but due to their unique chemical composition.5 As an alternative feedstock to ligno-cellulosic biomass,3 seaweeds contain a suite of functional ingredients, including polysaccharides, proteins, lipids and polyphenols. Indeed, seaweeds or seaweed extracts have been already marketed as valuable commodities for decades.6, 7 For instance, antioxidant and antimicrobial properties of polyphenol compounds in seaweeds promoted their applications as high-value green food additives.8 In addition, the presence of anti-methanogenic compounds and other nutritional components have encouraged the use of selected group of seaweeds or seaweed extracts as a functional additive to reduce enteric methane emissions from cattle as well as to enhance the yield of animal products.9 While the market share of direct consumption of whole seaweed biomass continues to grow in the global market,2, 10 current extraction processes of chemicals from seaweeds are focused on a single target product, such as hydrocolloids, colourants and phenolic compounds.2 For instance, the seaweed hydrocolloid industry primarily produces different grades of agar, alginate and carrageenan by processing over a million wet tonnes of seaweeds annually (mostly red and brown seaweeds, including Eucheuma, Gracilaria and Gelidium)11; and these seaweed polysaccharides generate over US $1 billion sales.12 Importantly, the culled, less valued biomass at harvest and leftover residues or effluents from post-harvest processing are usually treated as waste; however, these waste materials could be utilized as a raw material for the production of diverse bioproducts.5 For example, van Hal et al. proposed the production of butanol and biogas using seaweed residues through fermentation and anaerobic digestion, respectively.2 Furthermore, Kim et al. reported the co-production of alginate and biodiesel from a brown seaweed, Laminaria japonica, via the heterotrophic cultivation of an oleaginous yeast in the supernatant liquid from the autoclaved seaweed solution prior to the alginate extraction in the solid phase.13 Similarly, Polikovsky et al. demonstrated that hydrochar production can be combined with the co-production of ethanol, proteins and additional hydrolyzed monomers from 'waste' seaweed biomass.14 In addition, Cho et al. reported a substantial loss of edible seaweed Pyropia due to the discoloration caused by nutrient depletion or pathogens, and proposed the utilization of low-quality discoloured laver in the production of value-added compound.10 Therefore, a substantial amount of waste materials available in both seaweed harvesting and processing sectors could serve as an untapped resource with new market opportunities. In this opinion article, some of these waste materials generated from seaweed industry are discussed with the focus of initiating explorations of their potential as a raw material for the production of novel value-added products. Although recent studies have demonstrated intensified seaweed biorefinery approaches through which different constituents of seaweeds could be sequentially extracted or utilized via chemical, biological conversion processes,15 this cascading design of seaweed biorefinery demonstrated in the literature may not be readily adaptable in the developing seaweed industry due to capital costs associated with the assembly and operation of new conversion and separation technologies at scale.2, 14, 15 A more immediate and operable pathway towards discovering new valorization opportunity may lie in discovering utility of established waste-streams. Thus, a preliminary survey of available waste streams in the industry would provide information that could be relevant to developing flexible biorefinery concepts for the operation of large-scale seaweed processors, while maximizing the commercial potential of seaweeds. A recent FAO report indicates that the global seaweed production in 2019 was about 35 million tonnes in terms of fresh weight (more than 96% of the total production from aquaculture), and this production estimate accounts for nearly a quarter of the world aquaculture production.16, 17 Whether seaweeds are sourced from cultivation (i.e., onshore and offshore farms) or wild collection, it should be emphasized that a significant amount of waste materials are likely generated during harvesting or post-harvest processing steps.10, 15 Importantly, different waste materials may contain a number of valuable compounds that could be further processed, concentrated or refined to produce biofuels and bioproducts.3, 18 Figure 1 summarizes these waste streams based on our survey of local and overseas seaweed processing facilities; however, it should be first cautioned that harvesting and processing involve multiple steps and Figure 1 only considers simplified cases of extracting either hydrocolloids or other solvent-extractable valuable compounds (e.g., polyphenols) from seaweeds. In actual operation of industrial seaweed processing facilities, there could be other waste streams that are not denoted here. For example, liquid effluents following rinsing or blanching of edible seaweeds may contain functional compounds (e.g., phenolics, carotenoids) that could act as a plant bio-stimulant or be further processed to recover functional compounds.18 Nonetheless, seaweeds hydrocolloids in particular take up more than 65% of the overall world export of seaweed or seaweed-derived products (i.e., USD 1.74 billion in 201916) and thus the waste streams outlined in Figure 1 could provide a useful framework to consider when designing effective seaweed-based biorefineries. Four major waste streams identified based on our non-exclusive survey are: up- and downstream biomass residue generated during the production of hydrocolloids, residual biomass following the solvent extraction of harvested seaweeds, low-quality seaweeds (fouled or discoloured) or non-blade tissue types (e.g., stipes and holdfasts) culled at harvest (Figure 1). Although the availability of each of these waste materials depends on the species and quality of raw seaweed materials being processed and the choice of processing conditions at different facilities,15 literature survey and personal communications with industry experts indicated that these waste materials could be more than 50% of raw seaweed biomass. For example, during the production of refined carrageenan, roughly 75% of residual biomass (i.e., 3 times more than the amount of product being obtained, which roughly corresponds to E-factor = mass of wastes mass of products $$ \left(=\frac{\mathrm{mass}\ \mathrm{of}\ \mathrm{wastes}}{\mathrm{mass}\ \mathrm{of}\ \mathrm{products}}\right) $$ of 3 and Material efficiency = mass of products mass of products + mass of wastes $$ \left(=\frac{\mathrm{mass}\ \mathrm{of}\ \mathrm{products}}{\mathrm{mass}\ \mathrm{of}\ \mathrm{products}+\mathrm{mass}\ \mathrm{of}\ \mathrm{wastes}}\right) $$ of 0.2519) is generated and most of this residue is considered as waste with limited application as a soil conditioner (personal communication with Dr. Pete Bixler on 17 July 2020). Even though accurate statistics on the amount of each residual material generated from different seaweed processors are not readily available, partially because of the intricate supply chain of seaweed-derived products, the global export of major seaweed hydrocolloids (i.e., agar, carrageenan and alginate) alone was estimated to be 78,000 dry metric tons in 2021.20 Considering that the extraction yield of each of these hydrocolloids ranges from 17% to 52%, one could estimate that at least 72,000 dry tonnes of the residual seaweed biomass could be recovered from seaweed hydrocolloid producers alone, annually.21, 22 Similarly, the solvent extraction of functional compounds from seaweeds could generate a substantial amount of residual biomass, the relative portion of which could be greater than that observed during hydrocolloid recovery processes because of the lower content of these functional compounds than hydrocolloids within raw seaweeds.5, 15 In addition to the processing steps of harvested seaweeds, a significant economic loss due to biofouling or discoloration of seaweeds has been reported annually in seaweed farms globally, and the amount of low-quality biomass that does not meet commercial quality standards could be substantial. According to Sasuga et al., up to 16.7 tons of discoloured laver was deemed unfit for commercial use from November 2016 to May 2017 in Hyogo Prefecture, Japan, and seaweed producers did not harvest these low-quality seaweeds, which in turn caused environmental pollution in the area.10, 23 Likewise, in Seocheon-gun, South Korea, almost $20 million of economic loss was reported in 2011 due to the degradation of seaweed quality caused by nutrient depletion and pathogenic infection.10 However, even though these low-quality seaweeds are not suitable for human consumption, nutritional constituents available in the biomass may present opportunities for their utilization in different industrial sectors.24 While developing proper storage, distribution and additional processing technologies for liquid or solid waste materials needs to coincide with an extensive exploration of the commercial potential of different waste materials, Bixler and Porse recognized the stagnant growth of seaweed hydrocolloid markets and tightening seaweed supplies in recent years (i.e., a decrease in annual growth rate from 3% to 5% in 1990s to 1% to 3%).25 Therefore, aligned with the increasing recent interest in circular economies, diversifying product portfolio through the valorization of major waste streams in seaweed industry could turn out to be one of the major strategies to vitalize this industry by broadening market opportunities and possibly reducing environmental costs associated with waste being generated.14, 25 With the recognition of potential commercial value of waste materials from the seaweed industry, we have analysed three different seaweed residues generated during hydrocolloid or phenolic compounds extractions at commercial facilities. Specifically, the nutritional profile of these solid residues was analysed along with unprocessed dried seaweeds. Three residual samples included: residual biomass following solvent extraction of phenolic compounds from a Canada-based company (Residue A), filter cake waste generated from a carrageenan production facility in USA (Residue B) and filter cake waste generated from a carrageenan production facility in Philippines (Residue C). Additionally, three dried seaweed samples (i.e., rockweed [Ascophyllum nodosum], sugar kelp [Saccharina latissima] and Irish moss [Chondrus crispus]) were purchased locally; however, it should be pointed out that these seaweeds were not directly sourced from the facilities in which the above-mentioned residual materials were obtained. For instance, although A. nodosum was used as a feedstock for the production of the phenolic compounds in the Canada-based company, the sample of A. nodosum analysed in this study was not from the batch of seaweeds used in the corresponding residue. Furthermore, it should be noted that the companies that generated Residues B and C during carrageenan production use different strains of Eucheuma, a red seaweed subject to commercial farming in tropical regions. Therefore, direct comparison between seaweeds and industrial residues should be cautioned since factors other than extraction processes likely contributed to inter-sample (i.e., seaweeds vs. residues) differences. In addition to the strain of seaweeds, particular attention should be paid to seasonal variation in nutritional profile, which motivated studies aiming to preserve the quality of the harvested biomass by different processing/storage methods (e.g., blending after drying, ensiling).27, 22, 26 It is also important to note that the quality of different waste streams (including residual biomass and waste effluents) is presumably not the main concern of industrial-scale seaweed processors; each processor is likely to be operated under a site-specific operational protocol simply aiming to maintain the quality standards for the primary product. Therefore, different batches of waste streams could exhibit a substantial variation in its biochemical profile, reemphasizing the above-mentioned caution on making a thorough point-by-point comparison between residues and seaweed samples discussed here. Regardless, some of the parameters (e.g., ash, net energy value) that are far from the general values expected for seaweeds or feedstuff could still guide one to make an informed decision with respect to designing new utilization routes of the corresponding waste streams. The results of our preliminary analysis first indicated a substantial variation in terms of nutritional quality between different waste sources (Table 1). Notably, two filter cake (i.e., Residues B and C) wastes generated from hydrocolloid production, contained substantial amounts of ash (cf., 20%–35% of ash content in dried Eucheuma28), corresponding to the use of filter aid (e.g., perlite) during the separation of hydrocolloids. Compared with the residue generated following solvent extraction (Residue A), the high content of filter aid seemed to be a major cause of low to zero net energy values of these residues, thereby limiting their potential usage as a major nutritional ingredient. However, the amount of key trace elements (i.e., Zn, Fe and Cu) were greater in the two residues from hydrocolloid production than the residue generated from solvent extraction (Table 1).29 In addition, the amount of available protein (on a dry matter basis) in Residue B was more than double that in Residue A. Notably, Residue C exhibited the lowest amount of protein, indicating a variation in the quality of filter cake waste generated from each hydrocolloid producer. Comparison of the three residues with unprocessed seaweed samples indicated a reduction in the amount of nutritional energy in residues obtained following hydrocolloid extraction; however, three parameters of the energy content (i.e., NEL, NEM and NEG) of Residue A were determined to be greater than those of raw sugar kelp and Irish moss (Table 1). Therefore, while thorough comparison with the corresponding seaweed feedstock is needed, it could be tentatively concluded that residuals of seaweeds obtained following the solvent-extraction of phenolic compounds maintain favourable nutritional values, which may validate further investigations into their application in the feed and food sectors. Additionally, it could be speculated that the solvent extraction of phenolic compounds itself has a positive influence on the digestibility of the left-over residual biomass because of the physical disruption of cellular structures during the extraction, which in turn could better position them for feed and food applications than the corresponding raw seaweeds. Although a substantial loss of nutritional energy was observed in the residues recovered from hydrocolloid producers, which likely resulted from the inclusion of filter aids as well as generally rigorous physical and chemical treatments during the separation of hydrocolloids, it should be noted that these waste materials were recently promoted as a filter (i.e., additive) material in the fabrication of bioplastic or civil construction materials.22, 30 Given that a handful of industrial hydrocolloid producers we have personally communicated with indicated that much of these solid wastes are either disposed of as a special waste or distributed as an inexpensive soil conditioner to local farmers, exploring their new application routes as a more valuable additive seems particularly promising. While these new high-value applications would also include energy generation (e.g., biogas production via anaerobic digestion), the applicability of nutritionally low-quality residues from hydrocolloid producers in the feed and food sectors should not be completely ruled out. For instance, modest modifications to overall seaweed processing conditions could facilitate the application of the resulting residues in these sectors as well (see Section 4 for more discussion). As our preliminary evaluation of residual biomass indicates, the commercial potential of each waste material based on nutritional profile likely varies substantially depending on different extraction methods and possibly other operational conditions (e.g., seaweed species being processed, chemical reagents) at each facility. Notably, the filter aid used in the separation of hydrocolloids was reflected as a high ash content (>80%, DM basis); and its high inclusion could constrain the use of filter cake waste in nutritional applications. Regardless, a careful selection of filter aid could still justify the application of filter aid-rich residual waste in high-value applications, including ruminant or poultry feed as a potential source of buffering salts or micronutrient carriers.31, 32 For example, perlite is one of the commonly used filter aids in carrageenan production and a significantly positive influence of perlite on the weight gain of broiler chicks was observed at 1% perlite inclusion as a carrier for feed ingredients with no significant differences in feed consumption and mortality.32 On a related note, a recent statement released by European Food Safety Authority (EFSA) Panel on Additives and Products or Substances used in Animal Feed (FEEDAP) acknowledged the intended use of perlite as a technological feed additive for ruminants and poultry; however, EFSA panel clarified that it is not in a position to deliver an opinion on the safety and efficacy of the additive perlite as a feed additive.31 Therefore, other possible usages such as a carrier for anaerobic digestion systems or as a filler material for polymer compounding processes (e.g., biofiller for the fabrication of composites for 3D printing) may be more viable for waste biomass enriched with filter aid compared with the upstream residual waste without filter aid inclusion (Figure 1).33, 34 Similar to the residual wastes generated from hydrocolloid production, liquid or solid waste materials generated from harvesting or solvent extraction of seaweeds harbour different potential applications ranging from feed and biofiller to substrate for fermentation or anaerobic digestion (Figure 2). However, diversifying application of each of the identified waste materials beyond soil conditioner will require transdisciplinary efforts that address logistical challenges at industrial seaweed processors and a technoeconomic analysis to evaluate profitability. Furthermore, the lack of analytical methods and even standards for some waste materials calls for systematic investigations on available analytical methods assuring reliable and robust protocols. For instance, our understanding of possible shifts in the chemical composition of biofouled, discoloured or otherwise less palatable tissue types of seaweeds and its influence on the intended biomass usage through biorefinery approach is limited.10 In addition, although the waste materials obtained following milder treatment conditions are likely to maintain more functional ingredients, the degree to which each industrial process influences the availability of key seaweed constituents should be understood to guide further process optimization efforts. Given that existing literature on the seaweed biorefinery approach is focused on maximizing the value derived from seaweed biomass, with less emphasis on the sustainability of entire production chain,3 environmental costs and cradle to grave greenhouse gas generation associated with overall production processes should also be evaluated via life cycle assessment (LCA) as one of the key areas for future research. While continuing rigorous investigations into different biorefinery designs would remain as the key to achieving sustainable seaweed-based bioeconomy, we are convinced that carefully evaluating existing waste streams in the seaweed-based industry will serve as an important starting point for coming up with effective integrated seaweed biorefineries.35 Finally, based on the preliminary survey performed here, we hope that future studies can address important new questions one could raise, including: (1) How the practitioners and researchers in the field of seaweed aquaculture and utilization could openly share and track the available waste streams, while protecting their operational know-hows? (2) Which of the available analytical methods would be suitable as a standard protocol to effectively quantify the biochemical composition of seaweeds or seaweed-derived waste materials? (3) How much of a compromise (in terms of operational costs) each seaweed processor can make to transition from the conventional single product-focused operation to multi-product biorefineries? For example, can hydrocolloid producers use less efficient, yet non-toxic and feed-applicable filter aids to facilitate the use of the residual biomass as a feed ingredient? (4) How can different waste streams generated from seaweed processors be stored and transported effectively? (5) What kinds of industrial sectors could be synergistically operated together with different seaweed processors and provide new waste valorization or remediation opportunities? Jin-Ho Yun: Conceptualization; data curation; investigation; visualization; writing – original draft; writing – review and editing. Stephen Archer: Conceptualization; funding acquisition; investigation; project administration; resources; supervision; writing – original draft; writing – review and editing. Nichole N. Price: Conceptualization; funding acquisition; investigation; project administration; resources; supervision; writing – original draft; writing – review and editing. This work was supported by Shelby Cullom Davis Charitable Fund and the Agriculture and Food Research Initiative Competitive Grant no. 2021-69012-35919 from the USDA National Institute of Food and Agriculture. The authors would like to thank Dr. Harris J. (Pete) Bixler, who is a founder of Ingredients Solutions Inc. (ISI), a Maine-based company specialized in high-quality seaweed hydrocolloids, for sharing his valuable insights on seaweed hydrocolloid industry. Jin-Ho Yun acknowledges support from a grant from the National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF) as part of "Carbon to X Project" (No. 2020M3H7A1098291); a grant from the Commercializations Promotion Agency for R&D Outcomes (COMPA) funded by the MSIT of Korea (No. 2021B100) and KRIBB (Korea Research Institute of Bioscience and Biotechnology) Research Initiative Program (KGM5252221, KGM5402221). The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.
