AB  - In this article, we explore the first application of fuzzy cognitive maps (FCMs) for analyzing the variables of small-scale clay 3D printing processes, addressing an evident gap in methodologies within additive manufacturing (AM). While clay, as a sustainable and recyclable material, presents unique opportunities for customization in manufacturing, its inherent fragility poses significant challenges, including volumetric changes and structural instability. The proposed FCM methodology integrates advanced modeling techniques that extend beyond traditional statistical approaches, enabling a dynamic analysis of interrelated process variables. By systematically characterizing these factors, the study identifies critical parameters influencing print quality, contributing to the development of standardized best practices for clay AM. The findings underscore the potential of FCMs to enhance decision-making in process management and lay the groundwork for future research into optimizing 3D printing technologies with ceramic materials. This research ultimately aims to facilitate sustainable and efficient production methods in the context of circular economy principles. Furthermore, it may support the creation of guidelines, protocols, and standards that define optimal parameters and procedures for 3D printing with clay, thereby facilitating reproducibility and consistent quality across diverse production environments.
