AB  - Additive manufacturing (AM) technologies are currently attracting considerable interest due to their sustainability and versatility. AM allows the production of components, even those with very complex shapes, using only the strictly necessary material, thus reducing waste of resources. This potential can be maximised by exploiting the development of materials and coating deposits developed for the specific application. This is the context in which the present study is situated, which aims to demonstrate how component production can be optimized by exploiting the potential of AM technology, design, material development, and coating deposition, aimed at a specific application and with a view to sustainability, is presented in this context. In particular, the study presents an example of optimization of shape, materials, 3D printing parameters and coating, for the production of a component through the Fused filament fabrication (FFF) technology, using as filament a polymer composite consisting of a polyamide matrix (PA 12) and recycled carbon fiber (rCF)
