AB  - Powder reuse is becoming a central issue in Laser Powder Bed Fusion (LPBF), where material costs and process sustainability must be balanced with the strict requirements for part acceptance. This thesis investigates the effects of powder recycling on three key aerospace alloys — Inconel 718, AlSi10Mg, and Ti-6Al-4V — through a comparative literature review. The work was motivated by quality management tasks carried out during an internship in the 3D Printing and Additive Manufacturing Department at Rocket Factory Augsburg, where the evaluation of material test data and the part conformity with the Company's specifications demonstrated the importance of specific acceptance criteria for reused powder. The analysis shows that powder reusability is highly alloy-dependent. Inconel 718 maintains stable strength and density across multiple reuse cycles, but requires ductility monitoring. AlSi10Mg exhibits only minor changes in powder chemistry and porosity, but a consistent reduction in elongation makes ductility the limiting property. Ti-6Al-4V is the most sensitive to reuse, with oxygen uptake, formation of heat-affected particles, and increased roughness leading to noticeable losses in ductility and fatigue life, even when tensile strength remains stable. The findings from literature were then organized into a comparative risk matrix, powder acceptance guidelines and a decision flowchart to show how this data can support quality management decisions in the aerospace industry.
