KW  - Microstructure
KW  - 3D printing
KW  - Molding (decorative)
KW  - Fused filament fabrication
AB  - Additive Manufacturing (AM), commonly referred to as 3D printing, has emerged as a disruptive set of technologies capable of reshaping traditional manufacturing paradigms. Beyond its established role in rapid prototyping,AM now enables the production of functional and structurally demanding components with unmatched designfreedom, material efficiency, and production flexibility. This thesis explores the potential of consumer-gradeFused Filament Fabrication (FFF) systems to process advanced ceramic (Al2O3) and metallic (316L stainlesssteel) feedstocks, with the goal of bridging the gap between low-cost desktop printers and industrial powderbased manufacturing techniques such as Metal Injection Molding (MIM) or Binder Jetting.A systematic experimental program was carried out that included the optimization of printing parameters,chemical and thermal debinding cycles, and sintering profiles. The study investigated the effect of the cooling fanspeed, flow rate, retraction settings and infill density on part quality, interlayer adhesion, and porosity control.The debinding and sintering ramps were locally adapted to minimize cracking, minimize carbon residue, andachieve uniform densification in parts of different geometries. Comprehensive characterization of both thefilaments and sintered parts was performed: Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) and Energy Dispersive Xray Spectroscopy (EDS) confirmed homogeneous particle distribution, complete binder removal, and phasepurity; X-ray diffraction (XRD) validated crystalline phase stability post-sintering; and microhardness testing,density measurements, and dimensional shrinkage analysis quantified mechanical performance and densificationefficiency.The results demonstrated that near-theoretical densities and homogeneous microstructures could be achieveddespite the constraints of a desktop platform. Optical profilometry and SEM revealed a marked reduction insurface roughness and porosity after sintering, although anisotropic shrinkage (Sx ̸= Sy ̸= Sz) was observed,which required axis-specific scaling to achieve dimensional accuracy. Linear shrinkage of 20–22% and reproducible sintering-induced densification were achieved, with EDS confirming 0.0 at.% residual carbon in fullyoptimized parts. For 316L, tensile testing indicated mechanical strength within the lower bound of conventionally processed material, confirming functional viability for noncritical applications.In addition to the technical findings, an economic feasibility assessment highlighted the advantages of desktopFFF systems for research environments and SMEs, given their low capital cost (1,500-2,000 euros) and the abilityto support rapid design iteration. However, key limitations remain, including high material costs (€300–500/kgfor Al2O3), moderate success rates (60- 70%), shrinkage anisotropy and the need for controlled debinding andsintering environments to ensure batch-to-batch reproducibility.Overall, this thesis demonstrates that FFF can serve as a technically viable and economically accessible routefor pre-series production, prototyping, and small-batch manufacturing of metal and ceramic components. Thework contributes to the scientific understanding of the process-structure-property relationships in FFF, whileidentifying critical challenges such as surface finish optimization, shrinkage control, and process monitoring.Future directions proposed include the implementation of real-time sensors for intelligent cycle control, expansionof the material portfolio to other advanced ceramics and alloys, and integration of sustainability strategiesthrough solvent recycling, energy-efficient sintering, and life-cycle assessment. Together, these results andrecommendations pave the way for transforming desktop FFF from a prototyping tool into a robust, datadriven manufacturing platform for advanced engineering applications.
